# Copy to .env.local and fill in your Supabase project values.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-publishable-anon-key
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-secret-service-role-key

# Public site URL. Set this in production: OAuth callbacks AND the links
# inside the confirmation / password-reset emails are built from it. Without
# it the app falls back to the request Host, which behind a proxy can be an
# internal or preview hostname — a broken link, and a spam signal too.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://luckybronzecoin.com

# Google OAuth — same Client ID / Secret as in Supabase → Auth → Google.
# The server-side flow talks to Google directly so users never need to open
# supabase.co in the browser (fixes ERR_TUNNEL_CONNECTION_FAILED in some regions).
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-google-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-google-client-secret

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP — sends the sign-up confirmation email, the password-reset email and
# the contact-form copy. SERVER ONLY.
#
# ##  IMPORTANT: to stay OUT of the spam folder, SMTP_FROM must be an
# ##  address on a domain YOU OWN — never a free mailbox (@gmail.com etc.).
#
# Receiving providers judge a sender by the domain in the From header. Gmail's
# sender guidelines require SPF + DKIM + DMARC that line up with that domain.
# You cannot publish DNS records for gmail.com, so a From of
# "Lucky Coin <something@gmail.com>" can never be authenticated as this brand —
# and "a brand-looking automated mail arriving from an unrelated free mailbox"
# is the single strongest spam signal there is. The app logs a warning at send
# time when it detects a free-mail From.
#
# Recommended setup:
#   1. Sign up with a transactional email provider — Resend, Amazon SES,
#      SendGrid, Mailgun or Postmark. (Gmail SMTP is for personal mail: it
#      caps at ~500/day and carries no reputation for app-generated mail.)
#   2. Verify the domain luckybronzecoin.com there, then add the DNS records
#      it gives you:
#        SPF    TXT  @   "v=spf1 include:<provider> ~all"
#        DKIM   CNAME/TXT records supplied by the provider
#        DMARC  TXT  _dmarc  "v=DMARC1; p=none; rua=mailto:dmarc@luckybronzecoin.com"
#      Start DMARC at p=none, then tighten to p=quarantine once the reports
#      show SPF and DKIM passing.
#   3. Put the provider's SMTP credentials below, and set SMTP_FROM to an
#      address on the verified domain.
#   4. Verify with https://www.mail-tester.com — aim for 9/10 or better.
#
# Example (Resend):
#   SMTP_HOST=smtp.resend.com
#   SMTP_PORT=465
#   SMTP_USER=resend
#   SMTP_PASS=re_your_api_key
#   SMTP_FROM=Lucky Coin <no-reply@luckybronzecoin.com>
SMTP_HOST=smtp.your-provider.com
SMTP_PORT=465
SMTP_USER=your-smtp-username
SMTP_PASS=your-smtp-password
SMTP_FROM=Lucky Coin <no-reply@luckybronzecoin.com>
CONTACT_EMAIL=where-contact-messages-go@example.com

# Optional — sign outgoing mail with DKIM in the app itself. Only needed when
# the SMTP server does NOT sign for you; every provider listed above does, so
# leave these unset with them. Paste the private key with literal \n escapes.
# DKIM_DOMAIN=luckybronzecoin.com
# DKIM_SELECTOR=mail
# DKIM_PRIVATE_KEY=-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIIE...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----

# Optional — one-click unsubscribe endpoint. Only set this if you also send
# MARKETING mail: RFC 8058 requires an https URL, and advertising an
# unsubscribe on a login/password email marks it as bulk, which hurts.
# MAIL_UNSUBSCRIBE_URL=https://luckybronzecoin.com/api/unsubscribe

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stripe — the ONLY payment method on the /buy page. SERVER ONLY (never
# exposed to the browser: the client only receives the hosted Checkout URL).
#   STRIPE_SECRET_KEY      Dashboard → Developers → API keys
#   STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET  Dashboard → Developers → Webhooks → add an endpoint
#                          at https://YOUR-DOMAIN/api/stripe/webhook and
#                          subscribe to:
#                            checkout.session.completed
#                            checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded
#                            checkout.session.async_payment_failed
#                            checkout.session.expired
#                          then copy the signing secret (whsec_…) here.
#
# Currency: this is a Japan-only site, so every card is charged in JPY. Pack
# prices are listed in USD internally and converted at the fixed book rate of
# ¥150 per $1 (src/lib/fx-book.ts) — never a live market feed, so there is no
# currency environment variable to set.
# Note: Stripe rejects charges under ¥50, so the smallest custom amounts are
# declined with a clear message.
#
# This Stripe account may be shared with another product. Every session and
# PaymentIntent this site creates is stamped with metadata.app = "luckycoin",
# which is how the webhook ignores the neighbour's events and how the admin
# dashboard's payment history shows ONLY this site's income.
#
# Locally you can forward events with:
#   stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/stripe/webhook
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_your-stripe-secret-key
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_your-webhook-signing-secret
